13:43:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No — and this is actually the crux of the whole conversation, so let's nail it down clearly. **"Electric heating" isn't a single category with one cost profile — it's an umbrella term covering two fundamentally different technologies with opposite economics.** Grouping them together and asking "does electric heating win?" is a bit like asking "do cars win?" without distinguishing between a Prius and a Hummer — they're both "cars," but that category label tells you almost nothing about performance or cost. **Here's the actual breakdown:** - **Heat pumps:** Move existing heat from outside to inside using a compressor (similar mechanism to a refrigerator, run in reverse). Can produce 2-4 units of heat per unit of electricity. This is the technology driving the real cost advantage you're hearing about. - **Resistance heaters (baseboards, space heaters, most old-school "electric heat"):** Convert electricity directly to heat, 1 unit in, 1 unit out. No efficiency multiplier at all. These aren't two flavors of the same winning strategy — they're two different physical processes that happen to both be labeled "electric." The efficiency advantage that's making electric heating trend upward belongs *specifically* to heat pumps, not to "electricity as a category." A resistance heater doesn't get to borrow the heat pump's efficiency advantage just because both plug into the same outlet. **A clean analogy:** Imagine "boats" as a category, containing both sailboats and rowboats. If sailboats started winning races because of a new sail design, you wouldn't say "boats are winning as a category, so any boat — including the rowboats — should be riding that same advantage." The advantage belongs to the sail, not to "boat-ness" in general. So the answer to your question is: **no, a resistance heater doesn't get to ride the same cost wave as a heat pump, because the thing causing the wave (the compressor/heat-transfer mechanism) simply isn't present in a resistance heater.** It's genuinely a case where the category label is masking two very different underlying technologies, not evidence of a shared advantage.
