13:44:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really useful thing to bring up, because it points to something real: that "common sense" almost certainly wasn't invented out of nowhere — it was probably an accurate observation about *specific* appliances at a *specific* point in time, and it's the generalization over time and across appliance types that's introduced the problem. **A few reasons this kind of family wisdom often doesn't transfer cleanly to heaters specifically:** 1. **Old gas furnaces really were inefficient.** Decades ago, gas furnaces commonly wasted 30-40%+ of their fuel as heat that went straight up the flue. If your grandparents were comparing electric heat to a 1960s or 70s gas furnace, "electric is more efficient" may have been a completely reasonable observation *at the time*. Modern gas furnaces (90-98% efficient, condensing models) are a very different animal than what your grandparents may have been comparing against. 2. **The "electric is efficient" intuition may have come from other appliances**, where it's genuinely true — like cooking (induction stoves are dramatically more efficient than gas burners, since gas loses a lot of heat around the pot) or water heating in some configurations. It's very easy for a true observation about *one* appliance to get smoothed over time into "electric appliances in general," especially across a couple generations of retelling. 3. **Electricity generation efficiency has changed dramatically too.** Decades ago in a lot of places, and still today in some, electricity was and is generated by burning coal or gas at power plants — often at efficiency rates barely better than 40%, with additional transmission losses on top. So "electric" wasn't automatically the environmentally or economically superior choice even then — it just may have *felt* cleaner because the burning happened somewhere else, out of sight. 4. **Family heuristics are compressed from real experience, but compression loses nuance.** This is genuinely how useful practical knowledge gets passed down — someone had a real, specific experience (their electric range worked great, or their old gas furnace was a money pit), and it gets summarized as a general rule for the next generation because that's how people transmit lessons efficiently. It's not that your grandparents were wrong or careless — it's that "electric > gas, generally" was likely a reasonable shorthand for a real experience that had specific conditions attached to it, and those conditions changed (better gas furnace tech) or didn't fully apply to every appliance in the first place. So it's not that the family wisdom is being suddenly overturned — it's more likely that the original observation was true and specific, and it's the retelling over decades, generalized to "electric appliances" as a broad class, that's where the mismatch with modern heating technology has crept in.
