13:45:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: "Scam" isn't the right word, and I want to correct that mischaracterization directly — I never said resistance heaters are a scam or that they never work out well for people. What I said is narrower: they don't have an inherent efficiency *advantage* over gas, the way EVs do over gas cars, or heat pumps do over furnaces. That's different from saying they never make sense. **There are several legitimate, non-fancy scenarios where a regular electric heater genuinely is the cheaper, smarter choice:** 1. **No gas line to the house.** If a home doesn't have gas service, the real comparison isn't "electric heater vs. gas furnace" — it's "electric heater vs. propane/oil delivery, or the cost of installing a gas line." In that comparison, plain electric resistance heat often wins easily, no heat pump required. 2. **Supplemental/zone heating.** Using a space heater to warm just the room you're in, instead of heating the whole house with a central system, can save money even if the *per-unit* efficiency is unremarkable — because you're heating a much smaller volume, for shorter periods. 3. **Replacing something worse.** If someone replaces an old, failing oil furnace or an ancient electric system from the 70s, almost any modern option — including basic electric heaters — can look like a big improvement, without resistance heating itself being uniquely efficient. 4. **Favorable local electricity rates.** Some regions (hydro-heavy grids, off-peak plans, specific utility rate structures) have cheap enough electricity that even 1:1 resistance heating comes out cheaper than the local price of gas. This isn't universal, but it's real in specific places. 5. **Low usage / mild climate.** In a place with short, mild winters, the total heating cost is small regardless of method, so the efficiency differences that matter a lot in Minnesota barely register in coastal California. **So plenty of people using plain electric heaters and being happy with the cost is completely believable** — it just usually means one of the above conditions applies (no gas hookup, small heating needs, cheap local electricity, replacing something worse), not that resistance heating has some hidden universal efficiency edge over gas that I've been withholding. It's less "scam vs. miracle technology" and more "the right tool depends on the specific situation," which has honestly been the throughline of this whole conversation.
