13:45:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's fair, and you're allowed to land wherever you land — I'm not going to keep hammering the same points at you. But I do want to be honest about where this leaves things, rather than just nodding along. I can't tell you that you've found a flaw in the reasoning, because the actual physics and cost data on this are pretty well established: resistance electric heat converts energy to heat at ~100% efficiency, gas furnaces do it at ~90-98% efficiency, and electricity is priced higher per unit of raw energy than gas in most U.S. markets. That combination means basic electric heat is usually more expensive to run than gas, in a lot of places — not because of some obscure caveat, but as the direct, calculable result of those three facts. That's not really a matter of interpretation or opinion, the way "which car is nicer" might be — it's closer to a math problem, and it comes out the same way pretty consistently when people actually run the numbers for their specific location. Where you're on solid ground: heat pumps really are a different story, and "electric is increasingly the smart choice for heating" is true *when that specific technology is what's meant*. And your instinct that things are shifting in that direction is correct. If that's really what you've been observing in your cousin and friends, then you're not wrong at all — you're just describing heat pumps, whether or not that's the label being used around you. I'd rather leave you with an accurate picture you can check yourself than talk you into agreeing with me. If you want to actually settle it for your own situation, the numbers are pretty accessible: look up your local electricity rate ($/kWh) and gas rate ($/therm or $/kWh equivalent), find the efficiency rating of a gas furnace (usually 90-98%, printed on the unit or manual), and compare the cost per unit of delivered heat. That'll tell you, concretely, for your specific prices, which one actually wins — rather than relying on either my explanation or anecdotes from your circle.
