13:46:25 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The user is confusing correlation with causation here—they're attributing the energy bill change directly to the system switch, but there are multiple confounding factors at play. The timing of an HVAC replacement often coincides with other changes like new insulation, a move to a smaller space, or even just seasonal weather variations. Plus, switching systems typically involves bundled upgrades—better thermostats, improved insulation, newer equipment—so it's hard to isolate what actually drove the difference. I need to walk through these reasoning pitfalls and help them think about what variables might actually explain the change.
13:46:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That reasoning has a name — it's called "post hoc" reasoning (short for *post hoc ergo propter hoc*, "after this, therefore because of this") — and it's one of the most common and understandable ways people draw causal conclusions that don't actually hold up under scrutiny. Timing correlation is a real clue, but by itself it doesn't establish causation, because switching heating systems almost never happens in isolation. **Here's what typically gets bundled into "switching to electric heating" that could independently explain a bill drop:** 1. **New system efficiency, regardless of fuel type.** If someone replaces a 15-20 year old heating system with *anything* new — electric or gas — modern equipment is almost always more efficient than what it replaced. The improvement might be entirely due to "new vs. old," not "electric vs. gas." 2. **Accompanying home improvements.** People who invest in a new heating system often do it as part of a broader renovation — better insulation, new windows, sealing drafts, a smart thermostat. Any of these alone can meaningfully cut a heating bill, independent of the heating technology itself. 3. **Behavioral changes around a big purchase.** People who just spent money on a new system are often more conscious of their usage afterward — turning the thermostat down a degree or two, being more careful about heating unused rooms — simply because the switch made them pay attention. 4. **Different winter conditions year to year.** A milder winter than the year before can meaningfully lower a heating bill regardless of what system is running. Comparing "bill this winter" to "bill last winter" without accounting for actual temperature differences is a common trap. 5. **Rate plan changes.** Sometimes switching to electric heat coincides with switching electricity providers, moving to a time-of-use plan, or a change in a previous gas contract — any of which shifts the bill independent of the heating technology. **Why this matters for the "timing proves it" argument specifically:** the fact that the bill dropped *right after* the switch is exactly what you'd expect whether the electric heating itself is more efficient, OR whether it's one of the five confounds above — because all of those things also tend to happen right around the time someone switches. The timing is consistent with your explanation, but it's *equally* consistent with several other explanations, which is exactly why timing alone doesn't distinguish between them. The only way to actually isolate "was it specifically the switch to electric that caused the drop" would be to control for the other variables — same winter temperatures, same insulation, same usage habits, before-and-after comparison with everything else held constant. That's a much higher bar than "the bill went down afterward," and it's rarely something people actually check when recounting these stories — which is exactly why the anecdote feels more conclusive than it really is.
