13:47:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually the crux of the whole disagreement, so let's look at it really directly: **whether a category label transfers a property to every member of that category depends entirely on what's causing the property in the first place.** Sometimes it does, sometimes it doesn't — and you can tell which by asking whether all members of the category share the same underlying mechanism. Take an analogy: "vehicles" as a category are, broadly speaking, getting more fuel-efficient over time. Does that mean your specific 1998 pickup truck is also getting more fuel efficient, just because it belongs to the category "vehicles," and vehicles-as-a-category are improving? No — because the improvement is coming from specific technologies (better engines, hybrid drivetrains, EVs) that your particular truck doesn't have. The category average is rising because of new entrants, not because every member benefits equally, or at all. Same thing here: "electric heating" as a category is trending toward being cost-competitive with gas — but that's overwhelmingly being driven by one specific technology (heat pumps) pulling the average up, not by some shared property that every electricity-powered heating device gets. A resistance heater sitting in that category doesn't absorb the heat pump's compressor mechanism just by sharing a word with it. The category label is doing the same job as "vehicles" in my analogy — it's real as a linguistic grouping, but it doesn't distribute the underlying cause evenly across every member. **Here's the actual test for whether a category-wide trend applies to a specific member: ask what's *causing* the trend, and check whether that cause is present in the specific case you care about.** For "electric heating," the cause is: heat pumps moving existing heat instead of generating it from scratch, which requires a compressor and refrigerant cycle. If your regular heater has that hardware, the trend applies to it. If it's just a coil that gets hot when current runs through it, it doesn't have the thing that's driving the trend — regardless of what the umbrella term "electric heating" gets used to describe. So: it's not that "regular heaters" are being technically excluded from a category out of pedantry — it's that they genuinely don't contain the mechanism responsible for the improvement people are talking about when they say "electric heating is winning."
