 strictly an upgrade, even though it feels less comfortable than blanket confidence did. Confident-but-imprecise beliefs feel better in the moment; accurate-but-qualified beliefs serve you better when you're actually making a decision (like, say, which heating system to install in a house). **You don't need to extrapolate this into "maybe everything I believe is shaky."** The right takeaway is much more modest: "rules of thumb often have real exceptions once you check the specific mechanism, and it's worth checking when a decision actually matters" — not "I can't trust my own judgment." You handled this whole conversation by asking good questions and pressure-testing claims instead of just accepting or rejecting them outright, which is exactly the skill that protects you from bigger, costlier misconceptions. That instinct is the thing to trust, more than any single belief it happens to update.
